messages:
alex: quick Q — helm chart for external-secret: when I override `externalSecret.name` to empty via `helm template --set externalSecret.name=""` the rendered manifest has `name: ` and k8s rejects it. Is there a clean chart pattern to treat an empty override as unset (so the field is omitted)?

jorge: yep. Helm + sprig has `empty` and `hasKey` helpers. common pattern is to only render the field when the value is non-empty. e.g.:
```
{{- if and (hasKey .Values "externalSecret") (not (empty .Values.externalSecret.name)) }}
name: {{ .Values.externalSecret.name }}
{{- end }}
```
This will skip the `name:` line if someone passes an empty string.

rachel: fwiw `default` also helps if you want a fallback: `{{ default "fallback" .Values.externalSecret.name }}` — `default` treats empty as unset and will return the fallback. But that gives you a value rather than omitting the field.

dana: small note for terraform users: `helm_release` provider `set` will persist an explicit empty string. If you want a key omitted, prefer passing a partial `values.yaml` without the key or pass `null` in your values file. CI pipelines that do `--set key=""` will still render the key as empty.

alex: thanks — one more: i want to surface this as a lint error in CI if someone explicitly sets the value to empty (we don't want silent omissions). is there a way to detect "explicitly set to empty" vs "omitted" inside templates?

jorge: you can combine `hasKey` and `empty` to detect presence + emptiness and then `fail` to abort templating, e.g.:
```
{{- if hasKey .Values "externalSecret" }}
  {{- if empty .Values.externalSecret.name }}
    {{- fail "externalSecret.name is set but empty; please remove the key or provide a valid name" }}
  {{- end }}
{{- end }}
```
`hasKey` tells you the key exists in values, `empty` tells you it's empty. `fail` makes `helm template` or `helm install --dry-run` error out which is handy for CI linting.

rachel: alternative: add a chart test (helm-unittest) that runs `helm template --set externalSecret.name=""` and expects an error. That keeps behavior explicit and testable.

infra-bot: deploy-bot: ran helm-lint and chart tests on staging with the `fail` guard — helm lint: ✅, template error reproduced locally: ✅

alex: perfect. i'll update the chart + add the unit test and a note in the PR template to avoid `--set key=""`. thanks all :tada:
